WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.925
Running local inference begins with loading a compatible GGUF model and sending a prompt

00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:06.115
to the runtime.

00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:08.060
At first, keep the configuration simple.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.940
Use a known model, a short prompt, a reasonable output length,

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.735
and conservative context settings.

00:00:12.735 --> 00:00:15.750
The first goal is to prove that the model loads, the tokenizer works,

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.230
and the runtime produces output through the path you intend to use.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.235
After the basic path works, tune operational settings.

00:00:22.235 --> 00:00:24.970
Context length controls how much input the model can consider.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.080
Thread count can affect CPU performance.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.540
GPU offload can move supported model computation to the GPU when hardware and runtime

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.180
configuration allow it.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.560
Output length controls how much text is generated.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.160
Sampling settings influence determinism and variation.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.640
Observe prompt processing speed and token generation speed separately.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.240
A long prompt may take time before output begins.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.680
A large or poorly matched model may generate slowly.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.390
If the system swaps memory or runs out of VRAM, performance may collapse.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:54.695
A small benchmark based on your actual workload is more useful than a generic performance

00:00:54.695 --> 00:00:56.300
claim from a different machine.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.260
Save working command examples as documented configurations,

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.065
not hidden one-off experiments.

00:01:01.065 --> 00:01:05.360
Include model name, file path, context length, thread settings, GPU offload choices,

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.280
sampling settings, runtime version, and hardware notes.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.490
That documentation lets you reproduce a good setup, compare changes,

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.960
and recover when an upgrade or model swap causes unexpected behavior.
